            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house is pretty air tight, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through:





&lt;b&gt;o&lt;/b&gt;  The combustion air of the woodstove. I recommend to check the manual damper installed behind the woodstove. This should be opened when using the woodstove, and closed when it is not in use to reduce air infiltration. 



&lt;b&gt;o&lt;/b&gt; The weather stripping around the exterior doors. I recommend replacing the weather stripping around the exterior doors to ensure a tight seal. The back door (which was broken) does not close/lock tightly, however you have already identified this to be replaced. 



&lt;b&gt;o&lt;/b&gt; The basement ventilation fan. This fan is typically only used part-time throughout the year. During the seasons which the fan is not operational (such as during the winter), I recommend blocking the open fan, as it represents an open "hole" directly outside. Remember to open it when it is time to operate the fan again. 





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 350&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;15.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</airSeal>
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, there appeared to be minimal ventilation for your home, which was confirmed when you mentioned moiture/condensation has typically been an issue. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



Currently you have a whole-house exhaust fan, which draws air from each bathroom, as well as above the woodstove. As the infrastructure is already in-place, it may be possible to replace the whole-house fan with an HRV using the existing duct work, and returning fresh air through the heating duct work of the home. 



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an ENERGY STAR® rated HRV, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 80&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.







-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

If you switch to propane as a fuel source as noted above under the future potential boiler upgrade, I have modelled replacing your current hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your current hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 70&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; Savings are based on national average hot water use. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Current Hot Water Heater&lt;/b&gt;



Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly.

As you currently have a very efficient oil boiler, and it is already connected with a loop to the hot water tank, it would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water via this deticated loop. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your boiler to heat your hot water year round, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 200&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

As a future upgrade possibility when your boiler is nearing it's end, I have modelled replacing your current oil fired boiler with an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 330&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;44.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; By replacing your current setup with a condensing boiler, the flue and combustion air supply can be eliminated altogether. 





&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;







-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 750&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system, 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Woodstove&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your current woodstove for 50% of your heat load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 30&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 

&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; If you harvest your own wood, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 750&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.84 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $6.58 per bag



